   "text": "From opinion to evidence, because your instinct is where the question starts, but data is where the answer comes from. Because beliefs are not enough to invest time and money in building a product or a business idea. It also helps to go from expensive build to a cheap test. Anything that gives you the same answer as building the full product at, let's say, 1% of the cost and 5% of the time is always a better choice Then you go from certainty to a direction because you will never have guarantee or certainty. Because you will never have guarantee or certainty before you launch. So what this particular test promises is a direction, enough signals for you to take that next step with confidence. And then from launch day to the signal day. So you will not treat launch day as your first moment of truth after investing months and crores of rupees that now let's see whether the demand exists or not. No, that you should be doing just before that. The signal day with the lean validation, you already know that yes, I have much more confidence now after doing this test that yes, the demand exists. So 5 things from this lesson, or I should say 5 things from this topic is, the first is that lean validation is testing whether the real demand exists before you even invest in building. It is a structured test with a measurable and observable signal. The second point is the 3 traps that we learned about are the certainty illusion, the builder's bias and the speed fallacy."
