   "text": "was 12 months. For Zoko subscribers, it was 10.4 months. For Zoko one-time buyers, it was around 3 months. Third, we saw is the formula for the LTV results. So, we saw that Clairo, Zoko subscriber, Zoko one time had different kind of LTV results versus what they actually assumed because the longer observed lifespans. So, watch closely how lifespan affects your LTV in the real time. The fourth you learnt was LTV to CAC ratios. We saw how Clairo had a very strong LTV to CAC ratio versus Zoko one-time buyers, which had the weakest signal or it was in the warning zone. So, it was the most important signal for Zoko's subscription conversion priority. They should not focus on onetime buyers; rather, they should be focusing on the subscribers based on the intelligence that they got from the LTV to CAC ratios. And the fifth is that every LTV estimate contains 3 assumptions, that lifespan is stable, ARPU is stable or growing, and gross margin is stable. You name them, watch the signals that could break them, and keep a very close watch and look on all of them, so that you see that whatever LTV you have estimated, is it changing in the positive or in the negative direction? And you can change or you can take the corrective actions in the respective direction. So, now you have both numbers. CAC, what you paid to acquire one customer. LTV, how much they are paying to your company. And this ratio tells you the zone."
