   "text": "Now, most founders treat LTV as something that you calculate after 18 months of data. And of course, due to obvious reasons, because you would always feel that, \"Hey, how can I calculate that what is my customer's lifetime value? Is it 12 months or 18 months or 24 months? We have not even seen customers till that amount of time. We only have customers who are only 8 months old.\" So okay, we cannot calculate LTV, but it is an expensive mistake because without an LTV estimate, your CAC number is meaningless, and you will only have half the equation to decide on your growth equations or scaling your growth decisions. This topic will give you the other half and how you can actually have LTV even if you have very limited months data. And we're going to build it in Google Sheets, not just conceptually, with Clairo and Zoko's actual numbers. By the end of this topic, you will have a working LTV model. So, 5 outcomes from this topic. One, you will define LTV correctly and explain why you can estimate it early without having months and months or years of data. Second, you will apply the 3 input formula. Third, we will calculate LTV for our 2 brands, Clairo and Zoko. Fourth, we will identify the 3 assumptions inside every LTV estimate, and we will state what would break each one. Fifth, we will read the LTV to CAC ratio and state the implied growth decision. Now, outcome four is the one most experts skip."
