   "text": "Dropbox did is they redesigned onboarding to walk every user through a guaranteed first file sync experience. The folder syncing was the product's promised value, and hitting aha meant experiencing it once. Let's talk about Twitter. Their aha moment was whoever is actually joining Twitter should be following at least 30 accounts. Users who are having fewer than 30 followers had nothing in their feed. So, what happened is they opened the app, saw nothing, and then they left. Above the 30, the feed felt alive, and then the product worked. And let's talk about one more interesting case. If, like a lot of you would know about traveling, would know about Airbnb as well. And this is very interesting. So, they had 2 aha moments, of course, not for the same user, for different set of users. So of course, they have 2 specific kind of users, one who are guest, one who are host, who are hosting their properties. So the aha moment for guest was completing their first booking. The aha moment for the host was receiving their first 5-star review. So, we saw 5 companies, 5 different aha moments, but all have one common pattern. Each company looked at the data, found a behavior that's strongly correlated with the retention of those users. Then named that behavior their aha moment. And after understanding what is the aha moment, they designed the product to push every new user towards that aha moment. And that is the success playbook for any growth champion out there."
