   "lectureId": "BRGSA-M06-L04",
   "title": "Referral Mechanics",
   "text": "If a trust is high but rewards are wrong, the user wants to share, but maybe it stops before clicking because that zeal is not there. Or let's say if rewards are also right, but the mechanics are clunky. I'm like how they can exactly share. That is very complex or very difficult to share that. So the user might click, but let's say the friend will never sign up. When suppose if the mechanics are smooth and all of your other things are also smooth, let's say your rewards is fine, your mechanics is fine, but the trust is low, then it is the end of the game there itself because the user will never share. So trust comes the first, rewards comes the second, and your mechanics, like how people can share and how people can actually use it to sign up on your platform, comes the third. And most teams build in the reverse. They start with the mechanics. And which is why most referral features generally stay unused or people say, \"Hey this does not work.\" Because they have not designed it in a proper engineered structure. Now let's talk about the trigger moment and the question of when to ask for a referral. The right moment or the exact moment is just after the aha moment. You already know about the aha moment, what it is, how to take a user towards that. Now we exactly, so since we have already covered this concept in some of the previous topics or module. So the user has already experienced the first value."
