   "title": "90 Day Growth Roadmap",
   "text": "This topic is about the 90-day growth roadmap. How to take everything that this final module has built so far and turn it into a calendar that a real growth team uses every week. By the end, you will have constructed a 12-week roadmap for your chosen brand, and that roadmap is the centerpiece deliverable of the capstone. 5 things you will be able to do by the end of this topic. Explain why a backlog is not a roadmap and what the calendar layer adds. Apply the 3-month logic, validate inputs, run scaled experiments, decide and reallocate. Build a 12-week roadmap with 5 columns: week, active experiments, capacity committed, decision dates, and dependencies. Pre-commit the decision dates and review cadence before the quarter starts. And avoid the 4 roadmap failure modes that make plans collapse mid-quarter for most of the growth teams. First, the distinction between a backlog and a roadmap that we're talking about. Now, a backlog is not a roadmap. The 2 artifacts do different jobs. The backlog lives forever because it accepts new ideas continuously and is sorted by your ICE Framework score. It tells you what is worth doing. It says nothing about when and on what schedule and timeline. It says nothing about dependencies. It does not know about your team's capacity or the current quarter's plan. The roadmap actually lives 12 weeks because it resets each quarter. It is sorted by week, and it tells you what ships when."
