   "text": "Your targeting is already very sharp, and the changes whatever you're making in your marketing campaign, in your product, everything seems to be working better day by day, month on month. So, of the 3 shapes, the third one is the only one that justifies that, hey, you are into the right direction. Keep on scaling and scale fast.You can because you are having every signal right and correct. The other 2 requires a diagnostics in the first place. So, 5 things to take from this topic. The first, aggregate data tells you only the total thing. The cohort data tells you the truth underneath that total numbers. The total user count hides who stayed and who left. Two, cohort is the users grouped by a shared starting event and tracked over time. Entry event, time window, and metric. All 3 must be defined before your analysis begins. Three, Clairo's 8,400 total users look healthy in aggregate. But when you actually look into the cohort data, it shows that 78% are gone by month 3. So, the primary problem with Clairo is not is the retention and not the acquisition. When we looked at the Zoko's aggregate count, it actually hides 2 separate retention curves. Subscribers retain well and compound. One-time buyers do not return. And of course, each of them needs a separate kind of treatment in terms of your growth optimization. And the fifth, we understood about the cohort shapes, which matters as much as cohort size."
