   "text": "Then you can see the Zoko subscriber LTV to CAC ratio, which is 4.97 to 1, which is pretty strong threshold, very healthy, and this is worth acquiring more of. So, suppose if Zoko team says, and they want to increase their investments, then they will always focus that how we can actually increase these kind of customers more into our funnel. And similarly, we can see that the Clairo, they are actually declaring that as 7.5 to 1 and 13.3 to 1, which is very, very strong. Now, they just have to look at that, is my 12-month lifespan assumption is correct or not? So, they just have to keep looking at the signals, so that they do not just stay at those assumptions that, okay, fine, everything is stable. They just have to look at the signals and accordingly change their LTVs, and accordingly they can actually look at this LTV to CAC ratio on a month-on-month basis. So, the 4 zones on the right, below one-to-one is a danger zone. You should stop acquisition. You have to fix the product because your product or your business are actually losing the customers, and you will end up losing money If it is 1:1 to 1:3, then you are in the warning zone. Growth is possible, but it will be expensive and you actually have to see and break down your CAC through cohort basis, through channel basis, and then you have to identify which areas you can actually scale more so that you can improve this LTV to CAC ratio. And suppose if it is 3 to 5, let's say 1:3 or 1:5, then it is super healthy."
