   "text": "conversion rate might fall from 3.7% to let's say, 3%, 3.2% or maybe under 3, which can cost them thousands or millions of dollars. Let's consider the test of Zoko. Let's say, they started the starter kit CTA test. What CTA should work well? And since, they had a large number of traffic, let's say, they reach their sample size in 18 hours itself on a, let's say, on a weekday afternoon traffic. Now, the CTA will show that, \"hey, there is a 9% point lift.\" And the teams thought, \"Okay, that's wonderful. Let's run it.\" Now, actually the test should have run for a s, full 7-day week. The effect might drop to 2% points, and maybe the opposite could happen. But the team thought that, \"Hey, this is amazing. Let's go ahead with this.\" So now, there are two different causes, and both are type I errors. And both lead to constantly costly wrong decisions. Fine? So, always stay away from these kind of type I errors. Do not be too early in finishing the test. Now, let's talk about the type II error. There are 3 things to this. First, beta is the probability of a type II error, where the power equals 1 minus beta. So suppose, you consider 80% power, the standard default, there is a 20% chance that a real effect of the chosen MDE size will not be detected by your test. Little confusing? Let me put it this way. Suppose, we are talking about the type II error. So, it is that we are actually saying that there is certain change which actually exists, but we are not able to detect that."
