   "text": "Now, the point is they are also having decent amount of numbers, revenue of 6,200 customers, 72% subscription retention, as you can see in the brand bibles. So by most measures, Zoko is working. But here is the validation question this topic wants you to hold, which Zoko already did in the initial phase of building Zoko as a brand. Before manufacturing 3 collections of product, what Zoko did is they just ran an Instagram story ad investing around 5 to 10,000 rupees. They drove around 200 visitors to a test landing page. Mind it, test landing page, not a real landing page. That means a test landing page which shows the product but actually does not have. And they ran two versions. One ad spoke about 2X faster results, and one spoke about no chemicals, no compromise. And then they measured which is generating more clicks to their starter kit purchase page. The test took around 7 days, but then it told them what exactly people wanted because the test suggested that the 2X faster version is getting better result. So now they know that they will be building the brand around 2X faster results instead of no chemicals and the other thing. Of course, that is something they can keep as a secondary messaging. So lean validation is not just a set of techniques. It is a way of thinking and four shifts that separate the validation-first practitioners from the build-first founders."
