   "title": "Lean Validation",
   "text": "of test. But what you can do is identify your biggest assumption, and then you can reduce the risk around it. That, \"Hey, whatever I'm thinking, is that correct?\" One is, \"Okay, I think it is correct.\" There's 100% risk in that. When we do certain tests and then we feel that, no, now at least we are 70% more confident. So every experiment should target that single assumption that should I kill the idea or should I go ahead with that? Because the earlier you sense or the earlier you sense that there could be a failure, lesser will be the loss. The second is, the test would be cheap before investing a lot of amount of hard work on building that product. So suppose for a test, if you're just building a landing page, it will nearly cost nothing or minimal and maybe can be live in 4 hours. Wherein if you are building the complete product or business idea, it can take months of tech team or maybe some other team's time and huge cost associated with the same. Now, if the landing page can answer the same question that your product would answer after months, it is always intelligent to build that landing page or something similar first, instead of investing your whole energy, time and money on building the product for months. The third, you get direction over the certainty. Now, you're not looking for a guarantee here, as we said that we cannot get that. But we are looking for enough signals to take the next step with little more confidence."
