 "ibm_total_healthcare_cost_short_answer": [
  {
   "citation": "IBM-M02-L01#02",
   "lectureId": "IBM-M02-L01",
   "title": "Healthcare Focused Inclusive Business",
   "text": "And what is the state of associated services as far as healthcare is concerned, such as testing laboratories, which we called as diagnostics, such as medicines and about emergency response. If we need to understand healthcare and more specifically inclusive healthcare, then we need to keep all of these things in mind. Merely having free treatment at the hospital is not enough for providing inclusive healthcare. You will have to think of how a poor person reaches a hospital, how much money he or she is spending to even reach that hospital. How will a person who is unwell even reach a hospital? He or she can only do that if there is a caregiver, if there is somebody who is accompanying that person. So now we are not only talking of one person, but two people who needs to reach that hospital. Now remember, if they were not going to the hospital, what would these people be doing? They are poor people. They need to go and earn their livelihood. Which means that when somebody is unwell, and if you ask his or her brother or sister to accompany that person as a caregiver, then both of them are likely to lose their livelihood options, their income for the day. Even after meeting the doctor, there may be other expenses. There may be expenses to do some tests, such as X-rays or there may be expenses to buy medicines. And hence, as we go into this module, we will bring about the concept of total cost of healthcare."
  },
  {
   "citation": "IBM-M02-L01#03",
   "lectureId": "IBM-M02-L01",
   "title": "Healthcare Focused Inclusive Business",
   "text": "Think about all of those expenses that a patient incurs when he or she gets unwell. And if you have to make a healthcare model truly inclusive, then we have to work on each of these elements that contribute to the total cost of healthcare for a particular person. If you look at the Indian scenario, it is often said that health in India is a privilege, and it is not a right. You see the paradox in that statement. In a country like ours, healthcare should be a basic right. In fact, that is the promise of the government and the government keeps on making their effort in trying to make proper healthcare a right for every citizen. But unfortunately, because of various structural reasons, not everybody in this country can afford proper healthcare. It remains the prerogative of only the rich, and that's why it is said that healthcare is a privilege and not yet a right. Let me share some numbers with you. Almost every year it is said that thousands of women die in pregnancy during childbirth. The numbers have certainly reduced over a period of time. There used to be a lot more death of both mother and children during childbirth. Those numbers have reduced. But even today, the numbers run in thousands. It is said that approximately 1.7 million children die before even they reach the age of 5 because of various diseases and malnutrition."
  },
  {
   "citation": "IBM-M02-L01#01",
   "lectureId": "IBM-M02-L01",
   "title": "Healthcare Focused Inclusive Business",
   "text": "Today we are going to look at how to build inclusive businesses in the healthcare domain. As I had mentioned before, we will look at many such domains, healthcare, education, inclusive finances, or looking at the agricultural value chain and for each of these domains, we will have an anchor case study. The anchor case study for the healthcare domain is Vaatsalya Hospitals. But before we dive into analyzing Vaatsalya Hospitals as an inclusive business model, let us try to understand how is the healthcare sector as far as India is concerned, with more specific focus on how does healthcare matter for the poor and how do we deliver healthcare services to the poor. Here is a set of questions for you. What do you think are the biggest challenges when it comes to delivering healthcare in India? Since we are a large country of 1.4 billion people, we have a large demand for healthcare. Do we have enough capacity to deliver healthcare? And when you think of capacity, think of doctors, nurses, even infrastructure such as hospitals and clinics. Do we have enough reach so that healthcare can even be delivered to places that are remote? Remember that we are also a very large country and with a lot of diversity in terms of geography, which naturally poses challenges for accessibility. Can we deliver healthcare affordably so that even the poor can avail treatment when they are unwell? And as you will realize, that would be a focus of this particular course as well as this module."
  },
  {
   "citation": "IBM-M02-L01#05",
   "lectureId": "IBM-M02-L01",
   "title": "Healthcare Focused Inclusive Business",
   "text": "You can look at the numbers that they were about 10, 15 years ago versus what they were today. But the challenge still remains. There are still a significant number of people who do not have access to proper sanitation, who do not have access to proper quality food, and as a result, significant number of children, more from the rural background, are also underweight, malnutrition and hence they are likely to fall unwell, sick much earlier compared to the others. The government has different schemes. There are schemes to help mothers deliver child free of cost. There are schemes to get them to hospitals, because if deliveries happen in hospitals, then the chances of fatalities reduce significantly. Government is also working very hard in creating insurance because insurance is one product which enables even the poor to get proper treatment to pay for their illness if they keep on contributing a small amount all throughout the year. But many of these have implementation challenges, and as we go along, we will discuss some of those challenges. But to summarize, the problem is very big in India. We do not have adequate number of doctors, adequate number of hospital beds, and there are a lot of people who are still struggling to put together the money that they need to get a basic amount of healthcare. And we would like to look at inclusive business models as trying to address some of these challenges."
  },
  {
   "citation": "IBM-M02-L01#04",
   "lectureId": "IBM-M02-L01",
   "title": "Healthcare Focused Inclusive Business",
   "text": "We are unable to cure their diseases because their parents are poor and they also are susceptible to a lot of diseases because of the poor nutrition that they have. There is a certain ratio of physicians that needs to be available for treating a large population. The World Health Organization has determined this number to be one physician for every thousand people in the population. Unfortunately, we are way below that number. We are having about 0.6 physicians per 1000 population. So you see there is a reasonable amount of gap between the minimum number of doctors that we need to have to treat the entire population. The global average of number of hospitals per thousand population is about 26, whereas in India we are far, far lower. We are about 1 hospital beds per 1000 patients It is said that 26% of Indians have access to improved sanitation, which means a significant number of Indian population do not have access to proper sanitation. And why is it important? Because when you have improper sanitation, then it creates a lot of diseases. It creates a lot of chance for people to pick up infections. That's why there is a huge effort in India to create toilets, a huge effort in India to clean up our country from dirt and waste. Some of these challenges we will come and talk about later on, but you need to see all of these fairly connected. And once again, keep in mind that we have made significant progress in all of these counts."
  }
 ],
