 "ibm_low_cost_schools_short_answer": [
  {
   "citation": "IBM-M02-L13#02",
   "lectureId": "IBM-M02-L13",
   "title": "Indian Education Market",
   "text": "And for the purpose of this course, we will be focusing on which are catering to children coming from economically underprivileged backgrounds. It is estimated that about 92 million children are enrolled in half a million low-cost private schools. These schools also are of a very high variety. Some of them are run by individuals from their homes. Some of them may be a little bigger but they charge low fees. Sometimes these fees are even lower than the minimum daily wage, which is being earned by the parents of the children who come to these schools. Now the demand for such low-cost private schools in this country is likely to go up. Why do you think so? Simply because there is continuous large-scale migration from villages to cities. And cities, as we have seen, have difficulty in expanding the government schools, and the gap will only be met by private schools. At the same time, we also found that there is very poor public perception of the quality of teaching at the government schools. So, think about this. There is always a large population of people who are migrating from the villages to the cities. Many of them are coming with their families, with their children and these children do not have good schools to go to. They also are not coming from economically privileged background. So, they really need low-cost schools to go to."
  },
  {
   "citation": "IBM-M02-L13#01",
   "lectureId": "IBM-M02-L13",
   "title": "Indian Education Market",
   "text": "Now does, is there a very big market for private schools in India? Let us look at some of these numbers here. As we said before, there are about 250 to 300 million school-going children in India. And if you count the number of schools, there are about 1.5 million schools. 85% percent of the schools are located in rural India, and 75% percent are run by the government. What we have discussed so far will give you a fair bit of idea what kind of infrastructure and what kind of teaching happens in majority of these schools. As a consequence, the share of students going to private schools have been increasing, and it has almost reached 50%, which means that 50% of those 250 million school-going children today in India are going to private schools, even though there are large number of government schools in this country. Now, very interestingly, if you talk about private schools in developed countries, they will think of very expensive schools which can only be afforded by rich parents. But in India, these private schools actually cater to a very wide economic background. Children of migrant laborers go to the schools, as well as there are very high-end private schools catering to families who are economically much better off, sons and daughters of CEOs and rich businessmen. So, when we are talking about private schools in India, we are actually talking about a wide range of schools."
  },
  {
   "citation": "IBM-M02-L13#03",
   "lectureId": "IBM-M02-L13",
   "title": "Indian Education Market",
   "text": "And if there is not enough capacity in the government schools, or if there is the perception that teaching does not happen well in the government schools, then it can only be met by the private schools. How good are these private schools, by the way? And here the evidence is quite mixed. There is some indication that not many private schools, especially those that are run for poor children, have high quality. Good quality private schools would typically charge very high fees and hence that naturally takes them out of reach of the poor. On the other hand, there are some islands of excellence. For example, a series of studies made on private unaided schools run in the city of Hyderabad had found that these schools are of pretty good quality. They charge very low fees. They offer scholarships, but at the same time, they are able to teach the children enough so that they can get themselves enrolled in the regular systems as well as compete in the employment market. So yes, we have some examples of well-run private schools for the poor, but the estimate is that such examples are only few and far between. We had talked of the opportunity of the bottom of the pyramid market, which Professor C.K. Prahalad was talking about. Now, is there a very large BOP education market in India? The answer is yes. Why does the private sector not come and cater to it? Because that was C.K."
  },
  {
   "citation": "IBM-M02-L13#04",
   "lectureId": "IBM-M02-L13",
   "title": "Indian Education Market",
   "text": "Prahalad's dream, that if private companies are able to understand the bottom of the pyramid segment, then they will be able to offer products and services to this market, provided they are able to reconfigure their offering, understand the context, provide finance, provide information. Now, if that was to happen in India, it would have created a large opportunity for many organizations to come to this private sector for offering good quality education at a low price but that does not happen. Part of the reason is because this market is very geographically fragmented. Moreover, India is a very large country. While there are a lot of people at the bottom of the pyramid, there are also a significant middle-class market where parents are able to provide more fees. Parents are able to pay better fees and meet higher prices of the schools. And as a consequence, if an entrepreneur is running a school, he or she is likely to position it at the middle-class education market, where the paying capacity is a lot more than catering to the poor, where the paying capacity is typically much lower. One of the most profitable education segment in this country is the coaching market. That's where a lot of private sector has gone in because people are very keen to do good in the competitive exams. And as a consequence, that market is fully dominated by private players. Part of the reason also is that it is a fairly concentrated market."
  },
  {
   "citation": "IBM-M02-L13#05",
   "lectureId": "IBM-M02-L13",
   "title": "Indian Education Market",
   "text": "If you want to get coached for getting into an engineering college or for the IAIS exam, you are not likely to stay in your own village or a small town, but maybe travel to a bigger city like a Delhi or a Bangalore. And as a result, there are a lot of coaching centers in these cities, very concentrated, which enables them to become highly profitable. Whereas if you are running a school for the poor, you cannot have such a concentrated model. It will have to be in the small towns, in the villages, and that naturally makes your ability to make money much lower. We have seen this in the healthcare sector also. Moment you are thinking that the market forces will meet certain demand, when there is scarcity, the market forces typically push entrepreneurs to the most profitable segment. That is the reason that in the healthcare sector, you will find most private hospitals in the tertiary sector and in urban India. The same thing happens as far as education is concerned. The most profitable private schools are found in urban India catering to the higher economic segment, and as a consequence, you will find that the schools for the economically underprivileged to be severely underserved. This is where a social entrepreneur or a social enterprise must step in, whose objective is not to maximize profit, but whose objective is to create large-scale social impact by being financially viable."
  }
 ],
