   "lectureId": "IBM-M03-L03",
   "title": "The Classic Grameen MFI Model",
   "text": "When somebody borrows from a bank and does not pay back, that becomes a problem for the bank. That becomes a distressed asset. They track their parameters by saying that is a non-performing asset. So, if you judge a bank, they will always ask you what is the NPA or the non-performing asset. Now, you see that Grameen Bank's non-performing asset or the loans that were not paid back was very, very small, maybe today about 3 or 4% over such a long period of time. And this is often better than the best performing banks of the world, such as a Citibank or a Wells Fargo Bank. And that is the reason why Grameen Bank is almost like a poster child of inclusive business models. You can lend to the poor. You can make a lot of difference in their lives. You can lend to the poor. You can make a lot of difference to their lives by lending to them at proper interest rates, and yet you can actually build a profitable bank. Grameen Bank never needed to borrow money after their initial few years where the bank was getting established, and in almost all of these years, they turned profitable. They were never chasing the target of profitability. They wanted to be financially viable, so they would typically say, \"Our profits are modest,\" but that was the purpose, that you create significant social impact while you maintain certain amount of profitability, so that you remain financially sustainable. It is also interesting to note that Yunus always believed in lending to the women."
