   "title": "The Classic Grameen MFI Model",
   "text": "Lot of people later on asked him that why is it that you are very unfair to men and only lend to the women? In a funny way, he would respond saying that, \"No, I know that women are more trustworthy.\" But actually, the real reason was that in those setup in rural Bangladesh, if you think about it, it is the women who take a lot more responsibility of running the family, of taking care of maybe family animals, and as a result, they are much more stationary. Whereas it was possible that you, whereas it was possible that if you lend to a man, he may actually run away with that money. So, by lending to the women, Muhammad Yunus also minimized the risk of default, and his result actually shows that his assumptions was absolutely right. Grameen Bank has about 25,000 employees distributed across thousands of branches in Bangladesh. It is a great example of inclusive business model, and Yunus showed the world a particular model of lending to the poor. But at the same time, keep in mind that it is estimated that the number of potential micro-borrowers all over the world is almost 1 billion, with a total loan demand of about $250 billion. The present microfinance models serve only about 100 million people with $25 billion of loans being disbursed. So, it's a huge market out there, of which only 10% is being addressed, and hence it's a terrific opportunity for social entrepreneurs to step into this domain of microlending and build inclusive business models around it."
