   "text": "And the most important aspect of RuralShores was that it was very socially oriented. It's a classic example of an inclusive business model because the entities themselves needed to break even, they needed to become profitable. But at the same time, RuralShores was very sensitive about the fact that they had to involve the rural youth in delivering the services so that they are able to solve one of the tough problems of employment as far as rural India is concerned, because of these differences, the internal processes of RuralShores had to be also different. We have already learned that the employees of RuralShores were from rural background. They were coming from economically underprivileged past. They were recruited from villages within 10 to 15 kilometers of the center to minimize their travel. Most of them did not know English, and neither were they familiar with expectations in professional environment. Many of them had education up to class 10th or class 12th level. RuralShores made a conscious effort to recruit at least 50% of women employees. The foundation training that they provided to the employees was about familiarizing them with expectations in a professional setup. There was no night shift for the reason we discussed before, so they typically operated in two shifts."
