   "text": "Many of the employees were doing this job as a part-time job, which means RuralShores did not want to know whether these employees were involved in other activities such as farming, because so long the employees are coming to office and doing their job, they are fine with it. Now, this is a bit contrasted with urban BPOs, because in urban BPOs, if you're working, you are expected to do that on a full-time basis. RuralShores also was conscious about the socioeconomic setup in villages, and hence, they did not mandate any kind of interaction among all of these employees. If employees wanted to interact within their gender, that was their choice. It kept it voluntary. It allowed employees to naturally direct their own behaviour rather than mandating anything. What kind of impact is RuralShores creating? If you remember that, they took a conscious choice of employing at least 50% women in their organizations. And as a result, these women are empowered. Once they're empowered, they're able to decide about their future. They become the key financial earners for their families. And hence, it soon became at the interest of the family not to get these ladies or girls married off. They had a voice in their family. They could take decisions. And over a period of time, in the areas where RuralShores was operating, they found that the average age of marriage for these women were going up. Their families were indeed seeing value in what they were doing."
