   "lectureId": "IBM-M04-L04",
   "title": "The Rural BPO Model and Its Social Impact 2",
   "text": "The Rural BPO Model and Its Social Impact So, let us now summarize how the rural BPO business model was different from a typical urban BPO model. First of all, it is the location, that by definition, the location of rural BPO were in small towns or villages with less than population of about 40,000. That's what RuralShores chose to have. It is a decentralized model. Each of the centers would have about 100 to 200 seats, and hence they were operating from large number of smaller centers. The kind of services that they delivered would be regional voice, which means using local languages. They could also deliver multiple language services, and they were involved in a lot of transaction processing. This we had discussed before, such as cheque processing or looking at certain emails and trying to understand the problem that a customer was facing. So, keep in mind that many of them were very well-versed in reading English, but they may not be very good in communicating with English. Therefore, email analysis was something that RuralShores was able to do. In RuralShores case, all their clients were domestic clients because typically international clients would expect services to be delivered in English language, and that is something that RuralShores avoided. RuralShores also operated in a multi-ownership model, which means that some of the centers were owned by them, while some of the centers were administered by a third party, such as a franchisee."
