   "title": "The Rural BPO Model and Its Social Impact 2",
   "text": "Part of the reason is because it would have been very difficult for them to compete. Urban BPOs are very big. They are well-resourced. They are often part of large Indian or multinational enterprises. There was no point for RuralShores to go and compete for businesses. So, what they did was they went to the urban BPOs and said, \"Do you have certain businesses which, because of the pricing, are not worth for you people to pursue? Because the client is demanding such low price that you don't think that will be profitable for you. Give it to us. Since we are in rural India, we will be able to deliver those services and make the client happy.\" Thus, what happened was that RuralShores got into a partnership model with urban BPO rather than competing with them. Over a period of time, RuralShores also realized that they have some fundamental advantages because they're operating from rural India and because they were operating through multiple centers. Imagine, a setup where there is one center in Karnataka, one in Assam, one in Gujarat, and one maybe in rural Haryana. So, RuralShores will be able to provide multilingual support. They had a very strong IT backbone. So, if a call comes to RuralShores, and if the customer at the other end is talking in Hindi or Punjabi, then that call could be routed to Haryana. But if he or she was talking in Bengali or Assamese, then that call could be routed to the center in Assam."
