 "ibm_backward_integration_short_answer": [
  {
   "citation": "IBM-M05-L02#01",
   "lectureId": "IBM-M05-L02",
   "title": "Large Corporations Entering the Agricultural Supply Chain",
   "text": "The supply chain challenges which I just talked about are the ones which Reliance Retail wanted to solve. Now, most of you who are from India would be aware of Reliance Industries. It is one of the largest companies and one of the most valuable conglomerates, not only in India, but across the world. And they have a huge retail operation. They saw a huge business potential in this retail sector, where they thought that they should be sourcing agricultural products and selling them through the various outlets that Reliance Retail has. Their idea was that if they can improve the efficiency of the supply chain, then not only can they get the fruit or the vegetable cheaper, but they also can deliver better quality as well as enhance income from the farmers. Moreover, if they come into the retail sector and try to set up the supply chain, it also will have high potential for creating employment. Their initial plan was to have dedicated supply chains for fruits and vegetables. They are selling fruits and vegetables from their retail outlet, and they thought that they will create dedicated supply chain so that they can source it from the farmers. But later on, they decided that they will also supply these fruits and vegetables to other retailers."
  },
  {
   "citation": "IBM-M05-L02#03",
   "lectureId": "IBM-M05-L02",
   "title": "Large Corporations Entering the Agricultural Supply Chain",
   "text": "of bananas. Therefore, Reliance thought that if we are able to successfully create this efficient supply chain, then not only can we sell in India, but we can sell it all across the world. So, there was a high potential for exports. By the way, banana is one of the very frequent commodities which is exported all across the world. You should look up and find that which are the biggest exporting countries in the world for bananas. For a particular reason, they are also called banana republics. I would now want you to read the case and answer the following questions. What were the problems that Reliance was trying to solve? What were the key activities by which Reliance solved each of these problems? How do you think Reliance as a corporation, as an organization, was adding value? How would you measure Reliance impact? And also, what may be some of the unintended consequences of Reliance's intervention in the agricultural supply chain? Read the case and think of each of these questions before I discuss the answers with you."
  },
  {
   "citation": "IBM-M05-L02#02",
   "lectureId": "IBM-M05-L02",
   "title": "Large Corporations Entering the Agricultural Supply Chain",
   "text": "Since, they are going to the villages and sourcing fruits and vegetables, they were having a huge production, and they thought that they could actually give it not only for their retail outlets, but to even other retailers, both in the organized and unorganized sectors. And they chose banana as the first fruit for this experiment. Why was this an experiment? Because before this, Reliance had never tried to source fruits and vegetables for the farmers. They would receive it from a third party and sell from the retail outlet. So, this was a very new experience for them. Through their analysis, they found out that banana happens to be a fruit which has demand all round the year, and that is very important for business. If you have a commodity whose demand is uncertain, then it becomes problem for business to estimate how will I source it, how will I sell it? Now, banana, it seems, has a year-long demand in our country, and there are spikes in the demand when there is a festival season. India also happens to be one of the largest producers of bananas. I am told that we produce 25% of the global production"
  }
 ],
