   "text": "Can Businesses Truly Share Value? In an earlier module, we had talked about Professor Anil Karnani. When we had talked about Professor C. K. Prahalad and his thesis on Fortune at the Bottom of the Pyramid, we also talked about the alternate view which Professor Anil Karnani brought in. And later on Professor Karnani wrote another influential piece by the name Doing Well by Doing Good: The Grand Illusion. What was he talking about? There is in general a term in the industry which people said that you can do well by doing good, which means that you can benefit a large number of people, you can be inclusive in nature while you are being profitable. Very similar to what Professor C. K. Prahalad was saying. Now, Professor Anil Karnani came with a view that this is not possible under certain conditions. What was his argument? He said that consider a scenario where, when you are doing good to the society, for example, in this case you are helping to enhance the income of the small farmers. When you are doing it, as you are doing it, your own profit of the enterprise is also increasing. He said that if this is happening, it is perfectly fine. Why? Because for-profit enterprises need to enhance their profit and if while enhancing their profit it benefits the small farmers, it is within the business principles. But think of a scenario where to do good to the people you have to do certain things where your own profitability will come down."
