   "citation": "IBM-M05-L10#05",
   "lectureId": "IBM-M05-L10",
   "title": "SELCO s Sustainable Business Model",
   "text": "light. There was other instances where they realized that even if the house had 4 rooms, the people did not need to be simultaneously in all of those 4 rooms. So, what they needed was actually 4 bulb holders, but maybe 2 lights were enough, which could be very easily taken out from one holder and plugged into the another holder. And thus, a household can actually manage by buying only 2 lights instead of 4 lights. Now, when you build a culture like this where your people, your engineers, your salespeople are constantly thinking about the need that they're addressing for the customer, it is very difficult for them to go and ask for money, especially for loan repayment. And hence, SELCO, right from the beginning, saw a conflict of interest of themselves being both in the selling business of solar lights, addressing the needs of the poor, as well as to be in the business of financing. So, what was his solution? He said that, \"Yes, I know that the people who are going to buy my lights will need financing, so I will ensure that there are other institutions who can lend them money.\" SELCO's initial 4 and 5 years were spent a lot of time when they convinced the banks that the bank should provide financing for the lights. There was initially a problem because typically the banks would view lights as a consumption item, such as a television. And hence, they said that we only provide loans for income-generating activity, and we don't see a solar light as an income-generating equipment."
