   "lectureId": "IBM-M06-L04",
   "title": "Transforming Waste into Social Value",
   "text": "There was a gentleman, Lothfar, who originally moved from Delhi in 2010 to run a small garbage business, but he joined HDI in 2014, and very soon his franchisee owned multiple trucks, a scooter, and he started employing 22 workers. There is a story of Annamma, who was supported by the Hasiru Dala Trust, and he operated a dry waste collection center. She purchased her own truck, and with the additional income that she was getting from Hasiru Dala, she built a house, and she was able to send both her daughters to college. That's what we call as real impact. Without Hasiru Dala's help, probably her daughters would have never been able to go to college, and they would probably become waste pickers themselves otherwise. By 2020, Hasiru Dala Innovation had developed several entrepreneurs operating multiple trucks, and they were able to provide employment to more than 300 waste pickers. Some of this data is already given in the case study. But it just tells that how Hasiru Dala Innovation's interventions was able to create successful business models around the franchisees and also improve the life of the waste pickers. In order to increase their income, Hasiru Dala also participated in large scale events. You can think of it as income diversification. When there were large events such as weddings and corporate events or even big marathons in cities, which is quite common in a city like Bangalore, lot of waste is generated."
