   "citation": "IBM-M02-L08#01",
   "lectureId": "IBM-M02-L08",
   "title": "Aravind Eye Hospital",
   "text": "Now that we have understood how the Vaatsalya model was working, or at least what it was promising to do, let us look at certain other inclusive healthcare models. The first organization that we are going to talk about is Aravind Eye Hospital. It's a very large eye hospital today, but it had a very modest beginning in 1976. This was started by a very inspired doctor who is known as Dr. V, and his focus was to overcome the demand-supply mismatch that existed in cataract surgery in India. Cataract happened to be the most common cause of blindness in our country and it is very tragic because cataract can be removed and somebody who is suffering from cataract and has gone blind can actually be given back sight just by performing a very simple operation. So, what was the problem? The problem was that there weren't enough cataract surgeons in our country to do the kind of operations that are needed to meet the demand of cataract operations. It was said that cataract affected 2 million Indians every year, while the average cataract capacity, the surgical capacity, was almost half of that. Keep in mind that cataract surgery was free of cost in government hospitals. So, affordability was not a problem here, it was simply our inability to meet the demand. So, now when you have a supply constraint and price is not that a problem, then how do you overcome that supply problem?"
