   "title": "Impact Measurement Frameworks",
   "text": "But suppose some of you choose to become a social entrepreneur, then maybe I can take some credit for that. So those are medium-term effect. Why do I call it medium-term? Because merely trying to become a social entrepreneur doesn't mean that you have become socially sensitive. And hence, I will have to have also measure in the long term, which is what the impact measure. Now, this can happen through various means. It can be as simple as if you have extra money with you after you become financially self-sufficient, are you making those as donations to the economically underprivileged? Are you getting engaged with a lot of social activities in terms of your time, in terms of your quality? Are you sustaining the social enterprise that you have created, or did you close it down at the sign of first challenge? All of these are just examples, but it tells you that if I have to measure the social impact, the real impact of a social enterprise, then I will have to break it down into measurable metrics which spans across a very large span of time. And each of these metrics, you should be able to build a logic that class attendance leads to a project performance, better course performance, and only if you studied carefully enough, you will be able to participate in social events, and I can ascribe your participation to the fact that you attended my class. You can get into a lot of details, but I am just trying to tell you the complexity of measuring social impact."
