   "text": "knowledge, but the advantage is that you can have high degree of control. The center partner models seems to be solving many of these problems. The investment is brought by the center partner. It can hence happen fast. The center partner will also have local connections, but there are a few downsides. And this is exactly the set of problems which Selco had encountered when they set up their franchisee model. Who is your center partner becomes very important here. Why is he or she agreeing to run a rural BPO center? Can he or she have the same social orientation, or are they doing it only for getting returns on investment? So, one has to be very careful about selecting the center partner because your control over a center which is run by a third party is relatively low. Yes, you can set up the systems and processes, but monitoring them has high cost, and hence you will have to trust the partner. Now, do you have it in you to find out trustworthy partners who can keep your social mission alive? Otherwise, as we have seen in some of the unfortunate cases, that you may be able to scale through the center partner model, but there will be mission dilution, which means it will scale, but it will no longer remain an inclusive business model. The third option is about diversifying into other services. One of the challenges of rural BPO is that what kind of career can you offer to people? When they join the organization, they will have enough jobs to do."
