   "text": "Their tools are inadequate, and there is very little to distinguish between the work done by one person and another, such is the nature of the job. And all of this makes them having much low bargaining power. While they are very important for the urban economies, given the nature of the work and given that there are large number of them, they have very little bargaining power. They can demand very little. They are always at the receiving end of development of people who are privileged like you and me. As a consequence, this sector is characterized by very low productivity and low income. Their working days are long, their work is physically demanding, and as a consequence, they have very poor health conditions. There is also a very high degree of seasonal fluctuation in the demand of work, and which is one of the reason why they have to go back and forth between urban India and rural India. If there is enough work in urban India, they will be here. But then if there is harvesting work that is needed to be done in villages, sometimes they will migrate back. Once the harvesting season is over, they will again come back. Research indicates that while these informal sector workers are very mobile physically, they are very immobile economically, which means that their economic conditions do not change much."
