   "title": "SELCO s Sustainable Business Model",
   "text": "The push cart vendor, if he needs to operate at night, will buy a kerosene lamp. SELCO made the calculation and realized that this push cart vendor was spending almost 15 rupees daily to buy that kerosene. Hence, they figured out that if I can give the push cart vendor a light for which if he needs to pay me only 10 rupees a day, then it makes perfect financial sense for the push cart vendor to buy the solar light. And hence, this is how the whole financing model worked. SELCO would convince a bank to loan the money to the push cart vendor, and the bank would have a financing arrangement with the push cart vendor so that the vendor pays the bank back 10 rupees every day, which is like a microfinance loan. So, the push cart vendor buys the light, say for 5,000 rupees from SELCO. The bank pays the money to the SELCO, and the push cart vendor pays 10 rupees every day for a period of 2 years or 3 years as the structuring may be to the bank. How is the vendor gaining? Well, instead of spending 15 rupees a day for kerosene, he's now spending only 10 rupees a day, and now"
