   "text": "It improved productivity, it reduced the time spent in searching for sources of energy, and it also improved the productive hours of people who use those solar light. Not to mention that it improved quality of life, reduced health hazards, and it created greater possibility of education for the children. A very interesting thing about Harish was that right from the beginning, he was focused on creating a financially viable business model. He was trying to build his business in late '90s, somewhere around '97, '98. This discussion about building sustainable business models, addressing the needs of the poor, whether it was C.K. Prahalad or whether it was Muhammad Yunus, was not there at that point of time. So, he was one of those visionaries, he was one of those pioneers, quite like Professor Prahalad or Professor Yunus, who believed that it is possible to build sustainable businesses by addressing the needs of the poor. He would always say that the poor will pay for a product or service if the product or service actually addresses their real need, if the product or service creates value for them, and one does not need a donation or a charity-based model. He had seen firsthand that solar lights were being donated by the government. They would operate for 5 or 6 months in villages, but after that they would stop operating because of lack of maintenance. And this actually gave a bad name to solar lights. People would say that they have very short lifespan."
