   "text": "found out several ways of income enhancement. For example, a small eatery in remote Karnataka did not have connection to electricity, and hence they were only operating till sunset. Now with the solar light, his operating hours increased from 6 PM to 10 PM. And within those 4 hours, the restaurant would have enough income that would more than pay for the solar light, provided there was good financing that was bundled along with the product. There was also income enhancement through increase in productivity. For example, SELCO worked with people who were involved in plucking tea leaves. Now traditionally, they would hold one kerosene lamp in one hand and pluck tea with the other hand. What SELCO did was gave them solar lights which were fixed to their caps, which means that now both their hands were free and their productivity doubled. When you are picking up double the number of tea leaves in the same time, your income enhances, and with a proper financing model, that income should be able to pay for the loan that you have taken to buy the solar lights. Next, we would see a video where Harish talks about some of these innovations that Selco made, and after that we will discuss what insights we get from that. You find that replacing an expensive source of energy, which is also polluting, such as kerosene, can also pay for solar lights if there is a suitable financing option. And this is what we are trying to explain to you through this diagram. Think of a push cart vendor."
