   "title": "The Concept of Social Business",
   "text": "back to our earlier sessions about C.K. Prahalad talking about fortune at the bottom of the pyramid, he said that if you have to target a product or a service to the poor, innovation will have to be at the center of it. Innovation about material, innovation about the way you manufacture, about the way you distribute. And then to meet the price point, they also employed the principle of cross-subsidy that we learned in the course. Once they were able to bring this product to the poor children in Bangladesh, it naturally resulted in health improvement, and the many women that they employed in distributing the product also earned income which would lead to their empowerment. So, all of these was very beneficial for the poor in Bangladesh. But I had asked you one question, that how do you think this benefited the company itself? Did Danone benefit in any way apart from the fact that getting that satisfaction that they are doing good to the society? They indeed benefited because what Danone realized was that many people got very excited about their work. They were working in this company for several years, maybe work had become very routine. But suddenly when Yunus and the team went and told them that if you create this yogurt, and if this is distributed in rural Bangladesh, then it will make a real difference to the lives and livelihoods of the children and women there, then they got very motivated."
